Falsifies `line_editor::line_disc::verification::a_control_sequence_leaves_the_cursor_inside_the_line`.

The defect: the cursor-right move tests `cursor <= length` where it must test `cursor < length`,
and the search for the next character boundary falls back to one byte on where it must stop at
the end of the line. Together they let a right arrow at the end of the line step the cursor one
past the last byte. Either half alone is harmless, since the bounded search stops at the end of
the line, so the defect needs both; the unit test of a move off either end of the line also
catches it, by the move it echoes.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(editor.cursor <= editor.length_bytes)`, on a line whose
cursor sits at its end when the sequence `CSI C` arrives.

diff --git a/src/line_disc.rs b/src/line_disc.rs
--- a/src/line_disc.rs
+++ b/src/line_disc.rs
@@ -688,7 +688,7 @@
     }
 
     fn right(&mut self, out: &mut (impl Sink + ?Sized)) {
-        if self.cursor < self.length_bytes {
+        if self.cursor <= self.length_bytes {
             self.cursor = self.next_boundary(self.cursor);
             out.put(b"\x1b[C");
         }
@@ -732,7 +732,7 @@
     fn next_boundary(&self, at: usize) -> usize {
         (at + 1..self.length_bytes)
             .find(|&i| !is_continuation(self.buffer[i]))
-            .unwrap_or(self.length_bytes)
+            .unwrap_or(at + 1)
     }
 
